            'title' => 'A good sceptic has more than doubt and less than certainty',
            'image_alt' => 'A marble bust of a Roman orator in a dark museum, the face split by violet and teal light, an empty scroll resting loosely in an open hand.',
            'excerpt' => 'Massimo Pigliucci on Aeon brings back Cicero’s probabilia: a view firm enough to act on and loose enough to revise. In the clinic and in writing about artificial intelligence, that is more useful than bare doubt or certainty.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>On 21 September 2026 an essay by Massimo Pigliucci appeared on <a href="https://aeon.co/essays/a-good-sceptic-has-more-than-doubt-and-less-than-certainty">Aeon</a>. On the page it is titled <em>He was probably right</em>. The address says the same thing another way: a good sceptic has more than doubt and less than certainty. Pigliucci is the K. D. Irani Professor of Philosophy at the City College of New York. The same year he published <em>How to Be a (Happy) Skeptic: The Power of Doubt in a Meaningful Life</em>.</p>
<p>I read it as a physician, not as a historian of Rome. In the clinic, and in pieces about models, I keep meeting the gap the essay names. People know two postures. Either they doubt everything, or they have traded certainty for character. The vocabulary in between is missing.</p>
<h2>A word someone occupied</h2>
<p>Sceptic comes from the Greek <em>skeptikos</em>, the one who enquires. Since the 1990s it has been taken up by movements that deny settled knowledge. “Climate sceptic,” “vaccine sceptic,” “evolution sceptic.” The word sounds strict and independent, so it works as a shield. The difference shows in practice. A real sceptic changes their mind when a strong new piece of evidence arrives. The person who only borrowed the title moves the goalposts, raises a fresh objection, or picks the one study that fits. The conclusion was finished in advance.</p>
<p>On the other side stands a demand for certainty as the price of admission. “Follow the science” as a sentence that ends the conversation. Science does not work that way either. Both mistakes leave only two settings: nothing, or forever.</p>
<h2>What Cicero called probabilia</h2>
<p>Pigliucci takes Marcus Tullius Cicero as his model, and the school Romans called Academic Skepticism. From roughly 266 to 90 BCE that was how Plato’s Academy worked. Arcesilaus and Carneades concluded that the senses and reason are both fallible, so certainty is out of reach for a human being. They did not conclude that we should believe nothing.</p>
<p>Carneades had a Greek word, <em>pithanon</em>: persuasive, acceptable. Cicero translated it as <em>probabile</em>, plural <em>probabilia</em>. It does not mean probability in the modern statistical sense. It means worthy of approval. The claim has survived scrutiny, it fits what we have, and a reasonable person accepts it while remaining free to revise it. That is enough to act.</p>
<p>In the <em>Academica</em>, which Cicero, according to the essay, was finishing in early 45 BCE at a villa by the Lucrine Lake, the contrast runs like this: others declare that some things are closed. The Academics hold many things to be <em>probabilia</em>, which they can follow easily and can scarcely affirm. Follow easily, affirm rarely.</p>
<p>It is not one bin. Carneades graded impressions by how much scrutiny they had survived. At the first grade a thing merely seems more likely true than not. At the second it is persuasive. At the third nothing else you already hold conflicts with it. At the fourth it has been examined thoroughly. A trifle needs only the first or second grade. A treatment decision or a verdict needs the third and fourth.</p>
<p>The Academics differ here from the Pyrrhonists. A Pyrrhonist mostly suspends judgment and goes by custom and by how things appear. The aim is <em>ataraxia</em>, tranquillity. An Academic accepts the claim in a weakened but real sense, argues for it, and carries the cost if they are wrong. They are still after truth. They simply do not know whether they will ever certify it.</p>
<h2>What this looks like with a patient</h2>
<p>The value that a person should not be left without the treatment they need is held firmly. The claim that a particular drug, dose, or algorithm is the best instrument for that is an empirical bet. It is held only as firmly as the evidence holds.</p>
<p>When I write about <a href="article.php?slug=hypertenzia-oblicky-algoritmus-pre-vld">hypertension and the kidneys</a>, or about <a href="article.php?slug=lekar-ako-pacient-glp1-a-kortikosteroidy">what GLP-1 drugs and corticosteroids did to appetite</a>, I am not selling certainty. I am selling the best available account one can act on, and the conditions under which it should be rewritten. The same applies to <a href="article.php?slug=ai-modely-ako-nastroje-nie-operacny-system">artificial-intelligence models</a>. A model that passes a test is not the truth. It is good enough for a bounded task, and open to failing somewhere else tomorrow.</p>
<p>I take five habits from the essay into practice. They are not a quotation. They are what remains when the toga becomes a clinic.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Say how strongly you believe it.</strong> “I think X” is almost empty. “I hold X at about seven out of ten, and these three things stop me going higher” gives the other person somewhere to reach.</li>
<li><strong>Separate how much testing the claim has survived.</strong> Most of what we carry in our heads merely struck us as persuasive. Save confident action for what has passed a conflict with your other beliefs and a real check.</li>
<li><strong>Separate a value from a measurement.</strong> That a child should not go hungry is a value. That a particular school-meals programme reduces hunger is an empirical claim. When the two merge, a person defends a failing instrument as if their honour depended on it.</li>
<li><strong>State in advance what would change your mind.</strong> If nothing would, you do not have a belief. You have an identity.</li>
<li><strong>Act anyway.</strong> Uncertainty is not a reason to postpone a decision that cannot be postponed. It is the permanent condition. Acting inside it is a craft.</li>
</ol>
<p>The sixth habit hurts most in medicine. Error is data, not disgrace. If the method works, you will change your mind from time to time. The question is whether you take that as humiliation or as a sign that enquiry is running. The Academics had an exercise for this, <em>in utramque partem</em>: build the strongest case against your own view as well. Carneades did it in Rome in 155 BCE on justice, one day for it, the next day against it. The point was not cynicism. Until you know the best objection, you do not know what you actually hold.</p>
<h2>Science, dispute, and a fixed self</h2>
<p>Pigliucci applies this to three fields. In science, knowledge is not a collection of closed facts. It is a provisional consensus of the best-tested theories. Thomas Kuhn described the succession of paradigms. Imre Lakatos described why scientists rightly keep a research programme even with anomalies they cannot yet explain, and repair the edge before the core. Bayesian probability, developing the theorem Thomas Bayes set out in the eighteenth century, turns this into a calculation: starting points are updated by evidence, certainty does not arrive, and the strength of belief should be proportionate.</p>
<p>This is not relativism. Worse hypotheses fall away, better ones are sharpened. In 1942 Robert K. Merton described science as organised scepticism. Organised. Not a mood in which nothing holds.</p>
<p>In a democracy, persuasion is possible only when a citizen holds a political view as a <em>probabile</em>, not as dogma. Pigliucci points to analyses of votes in the United States Congress: ideological overlap between the parties narrowed from the 1970s and had effectively vanished around the turn of the century. Surveys by Pew and others since the 1990s show partisans more often holding a consistent ideological package and treating the other side as a threat to the country. Much of the shift is affective. On actual policy the parties have diverged less than they have learned to dislike each other. Evidence cannot get a grip on that kind of bond.</p>
<p>On a personal scale it is the temptation of a finished identity. A person who has found “their truth” and stopped asking is not wise. In the memoirs whose English edition, <em>The Prime of Life</em>, appeared in 1960, Simone de Beauvoir speaks of a conscious step into the lunacy called a philosophical system: tentative patterns become an obsession, as if they applied everywhere. A stiffened self that never updates is not character. It is rigidity in the costume of integrity.</p>
<h2>What I do not take from this as certainty</h2>
<p>Pigliucci also writes about the Catiline conspiracy in 63 BCE, when Cicero was consul, and about the executions the Senate approved, which later cost him exile. He deliberately does not say Cicero was right. He says Cicero acted on a judgment he knew might be wrong, claimed no certainty he did not have, and then defended the decision together with its cost. That is useful. It is not a release from consequences.</p>
<p>On the last words before the murder of 7 December 43 BCE, the essay offers a dramatic line. The ancient reports, Plutarch among them, are plainer: Cicero thrust his neck from the litter. Mark Antony had the head displayed, and the hands that wrote the <em>Philippics</em>. I do not need the exact last sentence. I need the method, not the legend.</p>
<p>The Republic fell anyway. According to the essay, Cicero went on acting as though it could be saved, because that was the best available view and it was worth acting on. The legacy remained in the books. Pigliucci’s closing sentence is also the title on the page: he was probably right.</p>
<p>If you are working through a similar gap between doubt and certainty in a clinic, a newsroom, or with a tool that promises certainty, write to me via the <a href="contact.php">contact form</a>.</p>
<p><em>This is a reading of Massimo Pigliucci’s essay of 21 September 2026, and of how I use it in my own work. It is not medical advice and not a history of Rome. The historical points I treat as checked are the date of death, 7 December 43 BCE, and Plutarch’s account of the execution. The other dating I take from the essay and mark as its reading.</em></p>
